{
  "id": "p20",
  "title": "Typed Files",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "p20-l1",
      "title": "file of",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "A text file holds lines of characters. A typed file holds whole values: var f: file of TBook; stores records directly, exactly as they are in memory. No converting to text and back.",
        "assign, rewrite, reset and close work like with text files. write(f, b) stores a whole record, and read(f, b) reads the next one back. readln and writeln don't work here: a typed file has no lines.",
        "Real database programs of the 80s, like the address books and stock lists of small shops, kept their data in typed files."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "program Library;",
          "type",
          "  TBook = record",
          "    title: string[20];",
          "    year: integer;",
          "  end;",
          "var",
          "  f: file of TBook;",
          "  b: TBook;",
          "begin",
          "  assign(f, 'BOOKS.DAT');",
          "  rewrite(f);",
          "  b.title := 'Algorithms'; b.year := 1976; write(f, b);",
          "  b.title := 'Pascal Manual'; b.year := 1974; write(f, b);",
          "  b.title := 'Turbo Pascal'; b.year := 1983; write(f, b);",
          "  close(f);",
          "  reset(f);",
          "  while not eof(f) do",
          "  begin",
          "    read(f, b);",
          "    writeln(b.title:15, b.year:6);",
          "  end;",
          "  close(f);",
          "end."
        ],
        "output": []
      },
      "exercises": [
